  <!-- About the Day -->
  <section class="section">
    <h2>About the Day</h2>
    <div class="fact-grid">
      <div class="fact-card">
        <div class="icon">💡</div>
        <h3>Origins</h3>
        <p>Take Your Child to the Library Day was created in 2011 by Nadine Lipman of the Waterford (Connecticut) Public Library. What started as a local initiative quickly grew into a nationwide movement embraced by libraries across all 50 states.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="fact-card">
        <div class="icon">🎯</div>
        <h3>Purpose</h3>
        <p>The day exists to remind families that libraries are free, welcoming community hubs offering far more than books — from story-times and maker-spaces to homework help, Wi-Fi access, and family-friendly events.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="fact-card">
        <div class="icon">📖</div>
        <h3>Early Literacy</h3>
        <p>Research shows that children who are read to from an early age develop stronger language skills, longer attention spans, and a lifelong love of reading. Libraries provide the perfect environment to foster these habits.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="fact-card">
        <div class="icon">🤝</div>
        <h3>Community Impact</h3>
        <p>Public libraries are among the most trusted institutions in any community. They serve as equalisers — giving every child, regardless of background, access to knowledge, technology, and creative programmes.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="fact-card">
        <div class="icon">🌐</div>
        <h3>Beyond Books</h3>
        <p>Modern libraries offer 3D printers, recording studios, coding workshops, board-game nights, and seed libraries. Many also lend tools, musical instruments, toys, and even Wi-Fi hotspots for home use.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="fact-card">
        <div class="icon">📊</div>
        <h3>By the Numbers</h3>
        <p>Americans visit public libraries more than 1.3 billion times a year — more than visits to movie theatres, theme parks, and major sporting events combined. Librarians answer nearly 6 million questions each week.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Fun Activities at the Library -->
  <section class="section">
    <h2>Fun Activities at the Library</h2>
    <div class="activity-grid">
      <div class="activity-card">
        <div class="flag">📕</div>
        <h4>Story-Time Sessions</h4>
        <p>Interactive read-alouds for toddlers and pre-schoolers, often with songs, puppets, and crafts.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="activity-card">
        <div class="flag">🎨</div>
        <h4>Arts &amp; Crafts</h4>
        <p>Many libraries host creative workshops — painting, origami, and seasonal craft sessions for all ages.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="activity-card">
        <div class="flag">💻</div>
        <h4>STEM &amp; Coding</h4>
        <p>Robotics clubs, coding classes, and science experiments introduce children to technology early.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="activity-card">
        <div class="flag">🎮</div>
        <h4>Gaming &amp; Board Games</h4>
        <p>Libraries stock board games, video-game consoles, and host game nights that bring families together.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- How to Celebrate -->
  <section class="section">
    <h2>How to Observe the Day</h2>
    <ul class="celebrate-list">
      <li>
        <span class="step-num">1</span>
        <div class="step-content">
          <h4>Visit Your Local Library</h4>
          <p>Take your child (or any young person in your life) to the nearest public library. Many host special events on this day with refreshments, giveaways, and guest readers.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="step-num">2</span>
        <div class="step-content">
          <h4>Get a Library Card</h4>
          <p>If your child doesn't already have one, sign up for a free library card. It's one of the most empowering things you can give a young reader — their own passport to knowledge.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="step-num">3</span>
        <div class="step-content">
          <h4>Let Them Choose</h4>
          <p>Allow children to browse freely and pick books that genuinely interest them — comics, graphic novels, fantasy, non-fiction, anything. Autonomy builds enthusiasm.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="step-num">4</span>
        <div class="step-content">
          <h4>Attend a Programme</h4>
          <p>Check the library's events calendar for story-times, craft sessions, author visits, or STEM workshops happening that day.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="step-num">5</span>
        <div class="step-content">
          <h4>Start a Reading Routine</h4>
          <p>Use the day as a springboard — commit to a weekly library visit or a nightly read-aloud at bedtime. Consistency turns a single visit into a lifelong habit.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="step-num">6</span>
        <div class="step-content">
          <h4>Share on Social Media</h4>
          <p>Post about your library visit using #TakeYourChildToTheLibraryDay to inspire other families and show support for your local library.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <!-- Why Libraries Matter -->
  <section class="section">
    <h2>Why Libraries Still Matter</h2>
    <div class="fact-grid">
      <div class="fact-card">
        <div class="icon">⚖️</div>
        <h3>Free &amp; Equal Access</h3>
        <p>Libraries are one of the last truly free public spaces. Every person — regardless of income, age, or background — can walk in and access books, computers, and programmes at no cost.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="fact-card">
        <div class="icon">🧠</div>
        <h3>Academic Success</h3>
        <p>Studies consistently show that children with access to libraries perform better in school. Regular library use correlates with improved reading scores, vocabulary growth, and critical thinking.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="fact-card">
        <div class="icon">🛡️</div>
        <h3>Safe Spaces</h3>
        <p>Libraries provide a warm, safe, supervised environment for children after school. Many offer homework help, tutoring, and mentorship programmes run by trained staff.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="fact-card">
        <div class="icon">🌱</div>
        <h3>Digital Literacy</h3>
        <p>Librarians teach children how to evaluate online information, use databases responsibly, and navigate technology — essential skills in the digital age.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

